<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lope de Vega</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1562">1562</a>–<a href="/years/1635">1635</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Félix Lope de Vega y Carpio</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lope de Vega was a Spanish playwright and poet, one of the leading figures of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-golden-age">Spanish Golden Age</a>. A prolific writer, he is said to have written some 1,500 plays, of which several hundred survive, including Fuenteovejuna and The Dog in the Manger. He shaped the Spanish comedia, the three-act play mixing tragedy and comedy that dominated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> of his time.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lope-de-vega.jpg" alt="Lope de Vega" width="320" height="411" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Attributed to Eugenio Caxés · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:LopedeVega.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Castile's Golden Age produced Miguel de Cervantes, <b>Lope de Vega</b>, Teresa of Ávila and Diego Velázquez.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crown-of-castile">Crown of Castile · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Spanish Golden Age produced Miguel de Cervantes’s Don Quixote (1605–1615), the plays of <b>Lope de Vega</b> and Pedro Calderón de la Barca, the poetry of Luis de Góngora and Francisco de Quevedo, and the paintings of El Greco, Diego Velázquez, whose Las Meninas dates from 1656, Francisco…</blockquote><a href="/empire/habsburg-spain">Habsburg Spain · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.42,-3.7&amp;year=1609" aria-label="Madrid on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4459 719 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Lope de Vega"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4909 989h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4909 989h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=40.42,-3.7&amp;year=1609">What was here in 1609? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…when I have to write a comedy I lock in the precepts with six keys, I banish Terence and Plautus from my study that they may not cry out at me; for truth, even in dumb books, is wont to call aloud…&#8221;</p><footer>The New Art of Writing Plays (1609) &#183; tr. William T. Brewster, 1914 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/76719/pg76719-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;But what can I do if I have written four hundred and eighty-three comedies, along with one which I have finished this week? For all of these, except six, gravely sin against art.&#8221;</p><footer>The New Art of Writing Plays (1609) &#183; tr. William T. Brewster, 1914 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/76719/pg76719-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…since the crowd pays for the comedies, it is fitting to talk foolishly to it to satisfy its taste.&#8221;</p><footer>The New Art of Writing Plays (1609) &#183; tr. William T. Brewster, 1914 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/76719/pg76719-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 535 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
